I can help — but first: what’s your intended use and environment? Are you trying to put your own site into maintenance mode, temporarily block access to a particular path or IP range on a server you manage, or do something else? Also tell me what server/platform you’re running (nginx, Apache, Node/Express, IIS, etc.). I’ll only provide solutions for legitimate, authorized administration of systems you control.

Below are safe, commonly used ways to “deny access” or put a site into maintenance mode that you can apply to your own servers.

1) nginx — return 503 (maintenance) with a custom page
- Purpose: temporarily deny access to users with a proper HTTP status (clients and search engines know it’s temporary).
- Rollback: remove or comment the config and reload nginx.

Example nginx server block:
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;

    # return 503 for all requests
    location / {
        add_header Retry-After 3600;
        return 503;
    }

    # serve the maintenance page when 503 is triggered
    error_page 503 /maintenance.html;
    location = /maintenance.html {
        root /var/www/html;   # change to your document root
        internal;
    }
}

Create /var/www/html/maintenance.html with the user-facing message. Reload nginx after changing config (nginx -s reload or systemctl reload nginx).

